/* book Worked example: Q: A two-cell box is 200 mm deep. Cell 1 is 200 mm wide and cell 2 is 125 mm wide. The outer walls are 1.5 mm thick and the shared wall 2 mm. It carries T = 20 kN·m, with G = 27 GPa. Find the cell flows, the shared-wall flow and stress, and the rate of twist.
   1. A₁ = 200 × 200 = 40,000 mm²; A₂ = 125 × 200 = 25,000 mm²; T = 20 × 10⁶ N·mm.
   2. δ₁ = (200 + 200 + 200)/1.5 + 200/2 = 500; δ₂ = (125 + 125 + 200)/1.5 + 200/2 = 400; δ₁₂ = 200/2 = 100.
   3. Equal twist: (500q₁ − 100q₂)/40,000 = (400q₂ − 100q₁)/25,000, which gives 33q₁ = 37q₂, q₂ = 1.1212q₁.
   4. Torque: 80,000q₁ + 50,000q₂ = 20 × 10⁶, so (136,061)q₁ = 20 × 10⁶: q₁ = 160.5 N/mm and q₂ = 143.2 N/mm.
   5. Shared wall: q₁ − q₂ = 17.4 N/mm, τ = 17.4/2 = 8.7 N/mm². Outer walls: τ = 107.0 N/mm² in cell 1 and 95.4 N/mm² in cell 2.
   6. Rate of twist: (500 × 160.5 − 100 × 143.2)/(2 × 40,000 × 27,000) = 3.053 × 10⁻⁵ rad/mm = 1.749°/m; cell 2 gives the same.
   Answer: q₁ = 160.5 N/mm, q₂ = 143.2 N/mm; the shared wall carries 17.4 N/mm (8.7 N/mm²); both cells twist at 1.749° per metre. */
