/* book Worked example 2: Q: A two-cell wing box, idealised into six booms that carry all the direct stress, has spars at x = 0, 300 and 800 mm and booms at y = ±100 mm: 1200 mm² on the front spar, 900 mm² on the middle spar and 600 mm² on the rear spar. The skins are 1.5 mm, the spar webs 3.0, 2.5 and 2.0 mm. A lift shear Sy = 60 kN acts at x = 200 mm. With G = 27 GPa, find the cell constants qs,0,1 and qs,0,2, the rate of twist, and the shear centre, using a program.
   1. Ixx = 2 × (1200 + 900 + 600) × 100² = 54,000,000 mm⁴, and Sy/Ixx = 0.001111 N/mm³.
   2. Cut the top skin of each cell. Each spar web then carries only the jump at its top boom: front −133.33, middle −100.00, rear −66.67 N/mm, positive downwards; the bottom skins carry zero (the bottom boom cancels the top one).
   3. Twist integrals of qb, anticlockwise: cell 1, −133.33 × 200/3 + 100.00 × 200/2.5 = −888.9; cell 2, −100.00 × 80 + 66.67 × 100 = −1333.3. δ₁ = 546.67, δ₂ = 846.67, δ₁₂ = 80.
   4. Moments about the front spar mid-point: the spar flows give 16,666,667 N·mm, the cell constants 2A₁qs,0,1 + 2A₂qs,0,2 = 120,000qs,0,1 + 200,000qs,0,2, and the load 60,000 × 200 = 12 × 10⁶ N·mm.
   5. The program puts the two twist equations and the moment equation into a 3 × 3 matrix and solves them.
   6. Final flows: front spar 147.72 N/mm, middle spar 100.31 N/mm and rear spar 51.97 N/mm, all upwards; they add to 300 N/mm × 200 mm = 60 kN, which checks. The skins carry 14.39 N/mm (cell 1) and 14.70 N/mm (cell 2), clockwise.
   Answer: qs,0,1 = −14.39 N/mm and qs,0,2 = −14.70 N/mm; the box twists at 0.134° per metre (clockwise, nose up, because the lift acts ahead of the shear centre); the shear centre is 287.4 mm behind the front spar. */
