/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In a stiffened skin most of the direct stress is carried by the stringers, spar caps and the strip of skin next to them.
   - Panels that keep part of their skin in the booms are called effective in bending, through the boom areas.
   - Take a skin panel of width b and thickness t between booms 1 and 2, with direct stresses σ₁ and σ₂ at its edges and a linear variation between them.
   - B₁ is the boom area at edge 1 contributed by this panel (mm²); t is the panel thickness and b its width (mm); σ₂/σ₁ is the ratio of the direct stresses at the far and near edges.
   - A boom collects one such term from every panel it touches, plus its own stringer or spar-cap area.
   - Ixx is the idealised second moment (mm⁴); Br and yr are the area (mm²) and distance from the neutral axis (mm) of boom r; σr is the boom stress (N/mm²); Mx is the bending moment (N·mm).
   - the jump in panel shear flow across boom r (N/mm) for a section symmetric about x; Sy is the shear force (N).
   - A closed idealised box is solved exactly as in the closed-section chapter: cut a panel, sum the jumps, add qs,0.
*/
