/* book Worked example: Q: A wing box has a rectangular mid-line 400 mm wide and 200 mm deep. Its four spar caps are 300 mm² each, and one stringer of 150 mm² sits at mid-width of the top skin and another at mid-width of the bottom skin. The skins and the spar webs are all 2 mm thick. Idealise it into six booms, check Ixx against the real section, and find the boom stresses for Mx = 30 kN·m and the panel shear flows for Sy = 15 kN through the vertical axis of symmetry.
   1. Skin panels are 200 mm wide (corner to mid-stringer) with σ₂/σ₁ = 1; each spar web is 200 mm deep with σ₂/σ₁ = −1.
   2. Corner boom: 300 + (2 × 200/6)(2 + 1) + (2 × 200/6)(2 − 1) = 300 + 200 + 66.7 = 566.7 mm².
   3. Mid-stringer boom: 150 + 2 × (2 × 200/6)(3) = 150 + 400 = 550.0 mm².
   4. Idealised Ixx = (4 × 566.7 + 2 × 550.0) × 100² = 3.3667 × 10⁷ mm⁴.
   5. Real section: skins 2 × 400 × 2 × 100² = 1.6 × 10⁷; webs 2 × 2 × 200³/12 = 2,666,667; caps and stringers (4 × 300 + 2 × 150) × 100² = 1.5 × 10⁷. Total 3.3667 × 10⁷ mm⁴: the same.
   6. Boom stresses: σ = Mx y/Ixx = 30 × 10⁶ × 100/3.3667 × 10⁷ = ±89.1 N/mm²: with Mx positive (top in tension in this convention) +89.1 in the top booms and −89.1 in the bottom booms.
   7. Shear: Sy/Ixx = 4.4554 × 10⁻⁴ N/mm³. By symmetry the top skin flow is equal and opposite either side of the mid-stringer, so the mid-boom jump 24.50 N/mm splits into 12.25 N/mm in each top panel.
   8. Each spar web: 12.25 + 4.4554 × 10⁻⁴ × 566.7 × 100 = 37.50 N/mm, and the bottom panels carry 12.25 N/mm. Check: 2 webs × 37.50 × 200 = 15,000 N = Sy.
   Answer: Corner booms 566.7 mm², mid booms 550.0 mm², Ixx = 3.3667 × 10⁷ mm⁴ (exact); boom stresses ±89.1 N/mm²; 12.25 N/mm in each skin panel and 37.50 N/mm in each web. */
